    793:| C3.a.iv (NOVELTY_CHALLENGES §1, S150 successor) — Arithmetic-primitive ABLATION of the C3.a bounded-Kt VM: are any of {LOG2, LI, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM} strictly necessary for the easy-zone cut shift documented in S150? | REFINEMENT | E | Composition of E5.8 (unchanged) + E1.3 (refined inline) + C3.a/S150 (refined). Re-ran S150's per-bit Kt_b'(s_J^(N)) scan over N ∈ {3,4,5,6}, J = 0..N−1 at L_max=28 under SIX ablation conditions: baseline, drop_LOG2, drop_LI, drop_DIV_LOG, drop_GEO_SUM, only_LI (drop other three). C inner loop (`sim_ablation.so`) extends `batch_enumerate_at_length_filtered` with a 16-bit `forbidden_ops_mask`; programs containing any forbidden op are skipped pre-simulation (exact ablation, no noise). Total 6 × 268M = 1.6B programs in 463s wall-time. **Pre-stated falsifiers (5 outcomes)**: F1 (LI solely necessary AND sufficient for easy zone); F2 (multiple primitives jointly necessary); F3 (LI alone insufficient); F4 (no single primitive strictly necessary); F5 mixed/incoherent. **Result: F4 holds for the easy zone** {(3,2), (4,2), (5,3), (6,4)} — every cell that compresses in baseline also compresses under every single-drop AND under only_LI. drop_LI matches baseline at L_max≤24 cells with alternative compressing programs: (N=3,J=2) `EMIT_S, PUSH_N, GEO_SUM, EMIT_S`; (N=4,J=2) `GEO_SUM, DIV_LOG, EMIT_S, PUSH_N, INC, PUSH_N`; (N=5,J=3) `EMIT_S, INC, LOG2, DUP, PUSH_N, PUSH0` (LOG2 alone); and is +1 bit at (N=6,J=4) (`EMIT_S, GEO_SUM, PUSH_N, ADD, DUP, LOG2, PUSH0` Kt 37 vs baseline LI-triple Kt 36). **Refutes S150's narrowest reading** that "iterated LI is the dominant compression mechanism" — LI is the cleanest realization but is one of four substitutable mechanisms. only_LI matches baseline on every easy-zone cell, confirming LI is sufficient; drop_LI also preserves compression, confirming LI is not necessary. The arithmetic-primitive class {LOG2, LI, DIV_LOG, GEO_SUM} forms an OVER-COMPLETE basis for E1.3-easy-zone bounded-Kt compression at the tested (N, L_max). **Hard-zone primitive sensitivity differs**: meaningful (L<target_len) cell (N=5, J=2) requires LI ∧ DIV_LOG (drop_LI saturates, drop_DIV_LOG saturates, drop_LOG2 and drop_GEO_SUM preserve) — the compressing program `DIV_LOG, LI, SHR1, SHR1, EMIT_S, PUSH_N, PUSH0` realizes a structurally distinct floor(out_count/log(LI(out_count)))>>2 mod 2 mechanism. The other "hard-zone" cells with apparent ablation-sensitivity (N=4, J=0,1) live in the L_max>target_len combinatorial regime (E1.3 caveat (a)) and are not load-bearing. **Closure mode E** (refinement of E1.3, no asymptotic improvement). **Refines E1.3 inline** with primitive-class-robustness statement: the S150 cut shift is driven by the FAMILY of slow-growing-integer-function primitives, not by LI specifically. **Refines C3.a/S150** by demonstrating that the optimum-program disassembly does not faithfully indicate which primitives are causally required. Cites E5.8 (unchanged), E1.3 (refined), E1.5, E5.3. Successors proposed (S158): C3.a.iv.α LI-vs-non-LI gap scaling at large N (test whether LI gains strict advantage at N≥7); C3.a.iv.β alternative primitive set {LN, INV_LI, ZETA_K} (test class-robustness across primitive sets). See `experiments/constructions/brandt_per_bit_arith_vm_ablation/`, `archive/sessions/session158_c3aiv_arith_vm_ablation.md`. | 158 |
    795:| §D45 ATTACK_VECTORS — Baker-Norine graph Riemann-Roch / chip-firing rank `r_G(D_P^N)` of the prime divisor on the divisibility graph `Γ_N := ([1, N], divisibility edges)` and the Hasse cover graph `H_N := ([1, N], cover edges)`: does `r_G(D_P^N)` deviate by `> 5σ` from a matched-degree random divisor, or exhibit Brill-Noether specialness `r > deg − g`? (cross-domain: Baker-Norine 2007 *Adv. Math.* 215, 766 = arXiv:math/0608360 + Dhar 1990 *Phys. Rev. Lett.* 64, 1613 burning) | FAIL | I | Frontier wild_swing S161 on the only chip-firing-theoretic invariant of arithmetic graphs. Implemented Dhar burning + q-reduction + Baker-Norine Algorithm 4.1 rank computation in `experiments/algebraic/baker_norine_chi_p/`. **Pre-stated falsifiers**: F1 (rank `r_G(D_P^N) ≥ deg − g + 2` for some N, A-grade Brill-Noether specialness witness); F2 (rank deviates from matched-density random by > 5σ — A-grade if mechanism is arithmetic; B-grade if reducible to graph topology). **Outcome (F2 holds in B-grade form, A-grade falsified globally)**: rank `r(D_P^N) = max(0, deg − g + 1) = 0` for all N ∈ {32, 64, 128} on both `Γ_N` and `H_N`. By Riemann-Roch `r(K − D_P) = g − π(N) − 1`, identical for any effective degree-π(N) divisor — **specialness in the Baker-Norine sense is the same for D_P and matched-density random divisors**. The rank invariant is determined by `(deg(D), graph G)` modulo Riemann-Roch and graph gonality — blind to deeper prime arithmetic. **Closed-form q-reduced-form identities (B-grade content, new EDGE E2.28)**: (1) on `Γ_N` with sink q=1: `D'_P^N = (π(N) − π(N/2)) · δ_1 + Σ_{p prime, p ≤ N/2} δ_p` (verified N up to 512); (2) on `H_N` with sink q=1: `D'_P^N = π(N) · δ_1` (verified N up to 512). Both proved via Dhar burn analysis. **Z-score growth on Hasse**: D'(q) = π(N) for D_P vs E[π(N)²/N] for random matched divisor → z-score `~ √π(N) ~ √(N / log N)` super-linear in N. Empirical: +8.6σ at N=32, +11.1σ at N=64, +14.6σ at N=128. **Mechanism for closure mode I**: D'(q) measures (chips on N(q) of D) for any divisor — a graph-NEIGHBORHOOD count, not arithmetic. For D_P on H_N, N(q=1) = primes (by Hasse construction), so D'(q) = π(N) tautologically. The chip-firing reformulation expresses π(N) as q-component of q-reduced D_P but is NOT algorithmically useful: building D_P^N requires knowing primes. **Generalised identity**: on H_N, q=1, for any divisor D supported on `{1} ∪ {primes}`: `D'(1) = D(1) + Σ_p D(p)`. Tested on D_P, D_sqfree (holds), D_μ_pos (holds), D_λ_pos (fails — chips on composites cascade through Dhar), D_Omega2 (fails — same cascade). **Adds EDGE E2.28** (chip-firing q-reduced identity for D_P^N on Γ_N and H_N, EVS M shape) — first chip-firing-theoretic invariant computed on any arithmetic graph in the project. **Distinct from**: CLOSED tropical lines 204, 326, 431, 432, 660 (those are min-plus compression of generating functions, fundamentally different from chip-firing on graphs); D22 CLOSED Hodge L_1 spectrum (linear spectral, vs non-linear divisor rank); A3 CLOSED Cayley graph spectrum (L_0 spectrum, vs higher-order divisor rank). **Cross-domain ingredient (Baker-Norine RR + Dhar burning) performed real work**: first project use of chip-firing graph theory; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §2 row "Baker-Norine graph Riemann-Roch / chip-firing" promoted PROPOSED → USED-I with edge E2.28. **Channelled mathematician**: Baker (graph divisor theory). **Successors proposed (S161)**: D45.a chip-firing on the prime-gap multigraph or prime-driven Cayley graph (where graph topology ITSELF is non-trivial arithmetic, not just `[1, N]` with divisibility) — does the rank invariant carry arithmetic content there? (1-2 sessions); D45.b "tropical Brill-Noether" — explicit dimensional study of `W^r_d(Γ_N)` Brill-Noether locus on arithmetic graphs (1 session); D45.c gonality of `Γ_N` and `H_N` exact computation (test whether arithmetic graphs have lower / higher gonality than typical sparse graphs of same edge density, 1 session). Cross-domain refs: Baker-Norine 2007 arXiv:math/0608360; Dhar 1990 PRL 64, 1613; Bjorner-Lovász-Shor 1991 Eur. J. Combin. 12, 283; Backman-Baker-Yuen 2019 Forum Math. Sigma 7. See `experiments/algebraic/baker_norine_chi_p/` and `archive/sessions/session161_d45_baker_norine_chi_p.md`. | 161 |
